# ==== ORPHAN-PRE-CLOSE · the NEGATIVE cells (AXIS B — over-consumption)
#
# The flush claims a PREFIX of the comment cursor, and membership is decided by
# COLUMN, not by the block's recorded span. Both halves are load-bearing, and
# both are only visible in the negative: a block's `span.end` is its DEDENT
# token, whose span reaches into the NEXT code line, so a span ceiling on its
# own drags the next item's leading comment into the closing block.
#
# CELLS (six):
#   1. a column-0 comment BETWEEN two containers
#   2. a column-0 comment LEADING the next item after a block
#   3. a comment at the ENCLOSING body's indent after a nested block closes
#   4. a comment after an INLINE suite
#   5. a comment at EOF, column 0
#   6. a tail comment in an EMPTY container (no child to anchor on)
#
# GREEN ON ARRIVAL — there is no pre-fix build to red these against, so each is
# RED-verified by BREAK-AND-WATCH instead:
#   * cells 1, 2, 3, 6 — relax the column test from `hash_col <= header_col` to
#     `hash_col < header_col`, or loosen the ceiling to the block's `span.end`
#     alone: the comment is dragged into the block above it.
#   * cell 5 — the same relaxation pulls the EOF comment into the last block.
#   * cell 4 — ⚠ NO CONSTRUCTIBLE RED. The `SuiteLayout::NextLine` guard on the
#     routed flush is DEFENSIVE: an inline suite's comments are already refused
#     by the column rule alone (the header line IS the body line, so the
#     comment is never indented past the header). Removing the guard leaves the
#     cell green. It is kept because the guard also stops a MID-LINE flush —
#     the inline-suite emitter routes through the same statement-body function
#     while the buffer sits mid-line — and that failure has no column test to
#     catch it.

struct A:
    int a
# BETWEEN — belongs to no container, stays at column 0

struct B:
    int b

# leading for the enum, at column 0
enum E:
    One()

struct Empty:
    pass
    # tail in an EMPTY container — no child to anchor on

void nested(int x):
    if x == 1:
        if x == 1:
            print(1)
    # after the NESTED block closes — belongs to the OUTER if body
    print(2)

void inline_suite(int x):
    if x == 1: print(1)
    # after an INLINE suite — belongs to the function body, not the if
    print(2)

void main():
    nested(1)
    inline_suite(1)

# EOF comment at column 0
